## --- AsyncReadWithTimeoutFuture (Lever D.2: persistent re-armed idle timer) --
##
## Wraps AsyncReadFuture's state machine plus a per-connection idle
## deadline. Resolves to:
##   * Ok(String)              — bytes (incl. zero-length on clean EOF)
##   * Err(IoError.<errno>)    — read(2) reported a real I/O error
##   * Err(IoError.TimedOut)   — `timeout_ns` elapsed with no readiness
##
## Two registrations are live while the future is parked, and BOTH are now
## owned by the parent AsyncTcpStream, not by this future:
##
##   1. The PERSISTENT read-readiness registration (installed at stream
##      construction, see async_net.c::ruxen_async_tcp_stream_from_fd).
##      park_current wakes on real bytes arriving — handled fully outside
##      this future. Unchanged from before Lever D.2.
##
##   2. The PERSISTENT idle-timeout timer (async_net.c::
##      ruxen_async_tcp_stream_ensure_timer). Previously this future
##      registered a FRESH reactor timer on first EAGAIN and deregistered
##      it on Ready/drop — under closed-loop keep-alive load the read
##      EAGAINs before the next request arrives, so that timer was armed
##      then torn down EVERY request (2 kevent syscalls on macOS just for
##      the deadline). Lever D.2 moves the timer onto the stream: created
##      once on the connection's first EAGAIN, RE-ARMED IN PLACE (same
##      reactor slot) on every subsequent read_with_timeout cycle instead
##      of a fresh register+deregister pair — 1 kevent/request instead of
##      2, identical timeout semantics. This future reaches it via
##      `stream_ptr` (the stream's own heap pointer, threaded through as an
##      Int like every other cross-poll-cycle handle in this codebase) and
##      never deregisters it — the stream's own drop/shutdown is the SOLE
##      timer teardown (async_net.c::ruxen_async_tcp_stream_unregister).
##
## Both registrations live in the same per-thread reactor, so
## `park_current` returns as soon as EITHER fires; the next poll
## resolves the race deterministically:
##   - If ruxen_async_tcp_read_step returns 2/3 (data/error), the
##     read won — return Ready with that result. The timer is left
##     exactly as it is (armed or not) — re-arming it is the STREAM's
##     job on the *next* read_with_timeout call, not this future's.
##   - If step returns 1 (EAGAIN), arm-or-re-arm the stream's timer and
##     check it. If fired, return Ready(Err(TimedOut)). Otherwise stay
##     Pending.
## Step is checked first so a byte that arrived in the same wakeup
## as the timer doesn't get silently dropped.
##
## State-struct lifecycle: on Ready-with-timeout we deliberately bypass
## ruxen_async_tcp_read_state_take_result (it would consume the buffer and
## lose the unit-tag semantics we need for the message-carrying TimedOut
## variant). The read state is freed unconditionally in `drop` via
## ruxen_async_tcp_read_state_free — this future's ONLY remaining
## teardown responsibility. It owns no reactor registration at all now.
class AsyncReadWithTimeoutFuture
  state_ptr: Int
  read_handle: Int
  stream_ptr: Int
  timeout_ns: Int
  armed: Int
  done: Int
  include Future
  type Output = Result[String, IoError]

  def init(fd: Int, max_bytes: Int, timeout_ns: Int, read_handle: Int, stream_ptr: Int)
    self.state_ptr = ruxen_async_tcp_read_state_new(fd, max_bytes)
    self.read_handle = read_handle
    self.stream_ptr = stream_ptr
    self.timeout_ns = timeout_ns
    self.armed = 0
    self.done = 0
  end

  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Result[String, IoError]]
    if self.done != 0
      return Poll.Pending
    end
    let w = cx.waker
    # Try the read FIRST, before touching the stream's idle timer. On a loaded
    # keep-alive connection the next request is usually already buffered, so
    # the read succeeds right here and we never touch the reactor at all. That
    # makes the fast path cost one read(2) and ZERO reactor syscalls — no
    # ensure_timer, no re-arm. A never-readable fd still reaches the EAGAIN
    # branch on its first poll and arms the timer there, so the timeout can
    # never starve.
    let step = ruxen_async_tcp_read_step(self.state_ptr)
    if step != 1
      # Ready (step == 2 data / step == 3 error). The stream's idle timer is
      # left untouched here on purpose — it stays armed (or unarmed, on the
      # very first request) until the NEXT read_with_timeout cycle re-arms it
      # in place. That re-arm-not-teardown is the whole point of Lever D.2.
      self.done = 1
      return Poll.Ready(ruxen_async_tcp_read_state_take_result(self.state_ptr))
    end

    # step == 1 (EAGAIN): no bytes yet, so we now need the deadline. Arm the
    # stream's timer exactly ONCE per read_with_timeout cycle, on the FIRST
    # EAGAIN of THIS future. Each keep-alive cycle constructs a fresh future
    # (armed=0), so the stream's persistent timer is re-armed once per cycle
    # (the Lever D.2 win: 1 kevent, not register+deregister). It must NOT be
    # re-armed on every poll: rearm_timer resets the deadline AND clears the
    # `fired` bit, so re-arming right before check_timer_fired would wipe the
    # very timeout we are about to observe and the read could never time out.
    # timeout_ns <= 0 still arms (reactor treats sub-ms remaining as "fire
    # ASAP"); user code wanting a zero-timeout poll should call `read` directly.
    if self.armed == 0
      let _armed_timer = ruxen_async_tcp_stream_ensure_timer(self.stream_ptr, self.timeout_ns)
      self.armed = 1
    end
    let timer = ruxen_async_tcp_stream_timer_handle(self.stream_ptr)
    ruxen_reactor_set_waker(ruxen_reactor_current_handle(), timer, w)

    # Check whether the timer has fired in the interim. If it has, timeout wins.
    if ruxen_async_tcp_stream_check_timer_fired(self.stream_ptr) != 0
      self.done = 1
      return Poll.Ready(ruxen_async_net_make_timeout_result())
    end

    # Neither side ready — park. park_current wakes on fd OR timer.
    ruxen_reactor_set_waker(ruxen_reactor_current_handle(), self.read_handle, w)
    Poll.Pending
  end

  def drop
    # The stream owns BOTH persistent registrations now (read reg + idle
    # timer) — neither is touched here, matching AsyncReadFuture's drop.
    # This future's only remaining responsibility is its own read-state
    # heap allocation.
    if self.state_ptr != 0
      ruxen_async_tcp_read_state_free(self.state_ptr)
      self.state_ptr = 0
    end
  end
end
